ch  is associated with autism and epilepsy  scientists then transplant those  organoids into the brains of baby rats  they found the neurons in these chimeras  had less branching and were smaller than  in chimeras with healthy organoids  remember that neurons and Dish bound  organoids are also less complex without  chimeras the researchers may have missed  this difference this highlights the  advantage of using chimeras for  understanding brain diseases and coming  up with new treatments that's why some  scientists are so excited about brain  chimeras but one big question is whether  human neurons actually hook up to rodent  neurons if they do you'd expect their  activity to affect the animal's behavior  the behavioral output is the ultimate  um proof that the organizer really part  of the entire system the same Stanford  researchers grew organoids with healthy  human neurons that had been tweaked to  have sensors that respond to Blue Light  they flashed pulses of blue light  directly into their chimeric rot brains  through a fiber optic cable this is  important the rats couldn't actually see  the blue light with their eyes the  flashes were literally all in their  heads by pairing the light pulses with a  sip of water the rats learned to expect  a drink whenever the light flashed that  lesson was wired into the human neurons  with the blue light sensors So  eventually when the researchers flash  blue light into those neurons the rats  when looking for water on their own and  what that shows is that the organoid had  become a working part of the animal's  brain the Stanford study only looked at  behavior in animals transplanted with  healthy human organoids The Next Step  will be testing behavior in animals  implanted with organoids from patients  with brain conditions and seeing whether  that's different that kind of  information would get scientists closer  to coming up with better treatments  chimeras are an incredibly useful tool  but they have some limitations 